answer:
Dynamic Routes (DRs) are used in Next.js to render dynamic content on a page without having to modify the page's layout or routing.
Here is an overview of how to use Dynamic Routes in Next.js:

1. Include the `next/routing.js` file from the `pages/` directory in your pages directory.

```javascript
import { useRouter } } from 'next/router';
import routing from '../pages/routing.js';

function App() {
  const router = useRouter();
  const slug = router.query.slug;

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Dynamic Routes</h1>
      <p>This example demonstrates how to use Dynamic Routes in Next.js.</p>

      <div id="dynamic-route" markdown="1">
        <h2>{{ slug }} - Example Dynamic Route</h2>

        <p>Welcome to the Dynamic Routes section of my Next.js application. This page will demonstrate how to create and implement a dynamic route using the `next/routing.js` file from the `pages/` directory.</p>

        <p>Let's begin by creating a new `DynamicRoute` component using the `next/routing.js` file from the `pages/` directory. To do this, we will first import the necessary components for our Dynamic Route component.
```javascript
import { useRouter } } from 'next/router';
```

We will then define the properties that make up the `DynamicRoute` component, such as the `slug` property, which represents the name of the dynamic route that is being rendered by the component.
```javascript
const { useRouter } } = require('next/router');

export default class DynamicRouteComponent extends React.Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = {
      slug: props.slug,
    };
  }

  render() {
    const { router, slug } } = this.props;

    return (
      <div className="dynamic-route-container">
        <h1>{ slug }} - Example Dynamic Route</h1>

        <p>Welcome to the Dynamic Routes section of my Next.js application. This page will demonstrate how to create and implement a dynamic route using the `next/routing.js` file from the `pages/` directory.</p>

        <ul className="dynamic-route-list">
          { router.query.slug
            ? (
              <li className="dynamic-route-item">{ slug }</li>
            )
            : (
              <li className="dynamic-route-item">slug</li>
            )}
          { router.query.title
            ? (
              <li className="dynamic-route-item">{ title }</li>
            )
            : (
              <li className="dynamic-route-item">title</li>
            )}
          { router.query.body
            ? (
              <li className="dynamic-route-item">{ body }</li>
            )
            : (
              <li className="dynamic-route-item">body</li>
            )}
        </ul>
      </div>
    );
  }
}
```

This component uses the `next/routing.js` file to render a dynamic route that takes the `slug` property from the URL query and returns the corresponding value in the `<li>`>` element of the dynamic route component.
You can use this component by creating an instance of it and passing the URL query object and dynamic route's slug property as arguments to the constructor. Then, you can render the dynamic route using its `<li>`>` element by accessing its `props` object which contains the URL query object as the first argument of the properties array, and then the second argument of the properties array is the `slug` prop of the dynamic route component, which will be used to retrieve the corresponding value in the dynamic route component's `<li>`>` element.
You can try this example by creating an instance of `DynamicRouteComponent` and passing the URL query object `query = { slug: 'example' }` as argument to the constructor. Then, you can render the dynamic route component using its `<li>`>` element by accessing its `props` object which contains the URL query object `query` as the first argument of the properties array, and then the second argument of the properties array is the `slug` prop of the dynamic route component `query.slug``, which will be used to retrieve the corresponding value in the dynamic route component's `<li>`>` element.

