To generate static parameters in a dynamic route using Next.js, you can follow these steps:

1. Import `generateStaticParams` from the `app/blog/[slug]/page` directory or by running the command `npm install @next/generate-staticparams` in your project's terminal.

2. Create a new React component called `DynamicRoutesPage` that imports the `generateStaticParams` function from `@next/generate-staticparams`.

```javascript
import { StaticParams } from '@next/generate-staticparams';

function DynamicRoutesPage({ post: { slug: slug } } }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{post.title}}</h2>
      <p>{post.description}</p>
      <p>{post.body}</p>
      <button onClick={() => generateStaticParams('Dynamic Routes', slug, post.type))}>Generate Static Params</button>
    </div>
  );
}

export default DynamicRoutesPage;
```

Now that you have created a new `DynamicRoutesPage` component in your `app/` directory, you can use the `generateStaticParams` function from `@next/generate-staticparamsparams` to generate static parameters for your dynamic route.

Here's an example of how you can use the `generateStaticParams` function with the `DynamicRoutesPage` component:

```javascript
import { StaticParams } from '@next/generate-staticparams';

function DynamicRoutesPage({ post: { slug: slug } } }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{post.title}}</h2>
      <p>{post.description}</p>
      <p>{post.body}</p>
      <button onClick={() => generateStaticParams('Dynamic Routes',slug,post.type))}>Generate Static Params</button>
    </div>
  );
}

export default DynamicRoutesPage;
```

In the above example, we imported the `StaticParams` class from `@next/generate-staticparamsparams` to generate static parameters for our dynamic route.

We then created a new `DynamicRoutesPage` component in your `app/` directory that uses the `generateStaticParams` function with the `DynamicRoutesPage` component's state.

Finally, we displayed the generated static parameters for the dynamic route using a button element that triggered the `generateStaticParams` function and returned the generated static parameters.

To use catch-all statements in Dynamic Routes, follow these steps:

1. Determine the dynamic route you want to create. This typically involves selecting a `route` property from your `components` array.

2. Create a new component that will handle the dynamic route. In this component, you can use catch-all statements as part of your route logic.

Here's an example implementation in JavaScript:
```javascript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'dynamic-route-catch-all',
  template: `
    <ul>
      <li *ngFor="route | catchAll;">
        {{ route }}
      </li>
    </ul>
  `,
  styleUrls: ['./dynamic-route-catch-all.component.css']}
})
export class DynamicRouteCatchAllComponent {
  constructor() {}

  static get routes() {
    return [
      { url: '', component: DynamicRouteCatchAllComponent } ]
    }
}
```

In this example, we create a new `Component` that will handle the dynamic route. Inside the component, we use a `catchAll` statement to match the URL of the catch-all segment.

The `catchAll` statement is enclosed in double square brackets and matches the URL of any catch-all segment.
```

